        "original_id": "cb8fe27d-2906-5f3f-917e-913c8e2d1cec",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Works: Allison Gross\n- Concepts: Rule of three\n- Characters: Allison Gross, the narrator, Maisry, the queen, the Seelie Court\n\nFacts:\n- In the ballad \"Allison Gross,\" Allison Gross is a hideous witch who tries to bribe the narrator to be her \"leman.\"\n- In \"Allison Gross,\" the narrator's sister Maisry comes to him to comb his hair.\n- In \"Allison Gross,\" the Seelie Court passes by one day, and a queen strokes the narrator three times to restore him to his proper form.\n- The rule of three is a writing principle that says events or characters presented in threes are often more humorous, satisfying, or effective and more engaging for readers.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
